WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:04.747
(slow piano music)

00:00:11.095 --> 00:00:13.987
- I walk into a room and I tell someone

00:00:13.987 --> 00:00:16.029
I'm a glaciologist and usually someone

00:00:16.029 --> 00:00:17.176
looks at me and says,

00:00:17.176 --> 00:00:19.142
well soon you'll be a historian

00:00:19.142 --> 00:00:20.960
because the ice is going away.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:22.069
We have the ability to turn this around

00:00:22.069 --> 00:00:23.416
and I think we're going to.

00:00:23.416 --> 00:00:26.299
We just have to start moving
in that direction now.

00:00:26.299 --> 00:00:27.416
The more we know about ice the more

00:00:27.416 --> 00:00:30.654
we can get onboard saving it.

00:00:30.654 --> 00:00:33.048
My name is M Jackson and I am a geographer

00:00:33.048 --> 00:00:37.215
and glaciologist and
National Geographic explorer.

00:00:38.144 --> 00:00:40.336
We have got great data that's saying

00:00:40.336 --> 00:00:42.815
the majority of the ice
worldwide is melting.

00:00:42.815 --> 00:00:44.747
It's responding to climate change

00:00:44.747 --> 00:00:46.300
with negative mass balance.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:47.588
There's a lot of different mechanisms

00:00:47.588 --> 00:00:49.041
but very generally we can say,

00:00:49.041 --> 00:00:50.662
Earth temperatures are getting warm

00:00:50.662 --> 00:00:51.847
and that's having a negative effect

00:00:51.847 --> 00:00:54.589
on the ice and the ice is getting smaller.

00:00:54.589 --> 00:00:56.641
The work I do really tries to

00:00:56.641 --> 00:00:58.157
address the question,

00:00:58.157 --> 00:01:00.756
what's going to happen
as the world's ice melts?

00:01:00.756 --> 00:01:04.423
Embedded in that question
is what can we do?

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.039
So, that's why I work along this

00:01:08.039 --> 00:01:11.039
whole south coast of Iceland region.

00:01:13.442 --> 00:01:15.265
The largest ice cap in Iceland

00:01:15.265 --> 00:01:18.653
and all of Europe,
Vatnajökull, is right here.

00:01:18.653 --> 00:01:20.840
Hundreds of years ago,
when say, I don't know,

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.456
the Taj Mahal was being
built, it was snowing here.

00:01:24.456 --> 00:01:28.039
And that snow was
turning into glacial ice.

00:01:29.024 --> 00:01:31.857
Okay, so when you're licking this,

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:35.231
what you're licking is snow that

00:01:35.231 --> 00:01:37.843
fell hundreds of years ago.

00:01:37.843 --> 00:01:39.969
And then town of Höfn is located

00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:43.432
where all of these glaciers drain down

00:01:43.432 --> 00:01:45.693
and so we can see the town right here

00:01:45.693 --> 00:01:47.838
and then you have all of
these different glaciers

00:01:47.838 --> 00:01:52.015
that are draining down into
the lagoons that surround Höfn.

00:01:52.015 --> 00:01:55.010
And what we see here is
that is a lot of complex

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.140
physical impacts of glacier melt,

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:00.963
and that there's a lot of
social impacts of glacier melt.

00:02:00.963 --> 00:02:03.648
Whenever we want to
visualize sea level rise

00:02:03.648 --> 00:02:05.709
and glacier melt, often times

00:02:05.709 --> 00:02:07.648
we are removing all the variables

00:02:07.648 --> 00:02:09.063
and we are just visualizing if

00:02:09.063 --> 00:02:12.824
all the oceans rose 10
feet, 100 feet, 200 feet.

00:02:12.824 --> 00:02:15.205
And the way that usually
looks is it's uniform.

00:02:15.205 --> 00:02:18.609
But that doesn't take
into account geography,

00:02:18.609 --> 00:02:22.782
places where the land is
maybe subsiding into the ocean

00:02:22.782 --> 00:02:25.032
or places that are marshes,

00:02:25.974 --> 00:02:28.360
or a really important one for anywhere

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.896
that there are glaciers,
land that is rising up.

00:02:31.896 --> 00:02:33.477
It's rebounding.

00:02:33.477 --> 00:02:36.343
We see a huge change
happening in the harbor.

00:02:36.343 --> 00:02:38.087
So if you imagine in your hand

00:02:38.087 --> 00:02:42.108
a sponge and you press that sponge down,

00:02:42.108 --> 00:02:43.634
when you take your hand away that sponge

00:02:43.634 --> 00:02:45.646
is just gonna spring right on back up.

00:02:45.646 --> 00:02:48.735
That is what is happening here.

00:02:48.735 --> 00:02:50.337
All of this ice that's here,

00:02:50.337 --> 00:02:52.152
it pressed the Earth's crust down,

00:02:52.152 --> 00:02:55.450
it pressed the island of Iceland down.

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:59.194
And so today as that ice is melting away,

00:02:59.194 --> 00:03:00.759
the land is springing back up.

00:03:00.759 --> 00:03:03.502
This is called isostatic
rebound, glacial rebound.

00:03:03.502 --> 00:03:05.531
These little islands
that are springing up,

00:03:05.531 --> 00:03:07.187
that's really great for the town.

00:03:07.187 --> 00:03:09.653
They pop a house right on
there, town got bigger.

00:03:09.653 --> 00:03:11.421
But what about the fishing?

00:03:11.421 --> 00:03:13.743
Fishing's a huge part
of the Icelandic economy

00:03:13.743 --> 00:03:16.501
but there's just this one
harbor in this whole area.

00:03:16.501 --> 00:03:18.627
It's getting shallower, and shallower,

00:03:18.627 --> 00:03:19.794
and shallower.

00:03:19.794 --> 00:03:21.653
Boats have to sit outside of the harbor

00:03:21.653 --> 00:03:24.042
and wait for high tide.

00:03:24.042 --> 00:03:26.367
What's gonna happen to
the fish-packing plant?

00:03:26.367 --> 00:03:27.508
What's gonna happen to the biggest

00:03:27.508 --> 00:03:29.068
industry in this town?

00:03:29.068 --> 00:03:33.127
Tourism is the second growing industry.

00:03:33.127 --> 00:03:35.349
There used to be no real
glacier tours in here

00:03:35.349 --> 00:03:37.104
and suddenly people
from all over the world,

00:03:37.104 --> 00:03:40.137
they're coming to see the
glaciers before they're gone,

00:03:40.137 --> 00:03:43.845
but also they're coming to
be inside these ice caves.

00:03:43.845 --> 00:03:46.387
Icelanders didn't really have
a business in the winter.

00:03:46.387 --> 00:03:47.704
They now have jobs in the winter,

00:03:47.704 --> 00:03:49.822
they have guiding into these ice caves.

00:03:49.822 --> 00:03:53.940
They're making livings and
livelihoods with these.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:55.890
But the hard bit about this is that

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:58.585
a lot of these ice caves are made

00:03:58.585 --> 00:04:00.163
because glaciers are melting quicker.

00:04:00.163 --> 00:04:02.111
That's the paradox.

00:04:02.111 --> 00:04:03.783
I look at all of of
that physical geography

00:04:03.783 --> 00:04:06.576
aspects of ice and I add
all the social aspects.

00:04:06.576 --> 00:04:08.269
We put it together, we get a whole picture

00:04:08.269 --> 00:04:10.504
of what's happening.

00:04:10.504 --> 00:04:12.921
The oceans are so, so vast,

00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:17.733
but they are often controlled
and shaped by glaciers.

00:04:17.733 --> 00:04:21.398
So if you are standing,
looking at your coastline,

00:04:21.398 --> 00:04:23.356
during our last ice age the ocean

00:04:23.356 --> 00:04:27.123
was about 400 feet lower than it is today.

00:04:27.123 --> 00:04:29.494
That's because those glaciers,

00:04:29.494 --> 00:04:33.494
they held more of that
water in the form of ice.

00:04:34.399 --> 00:04:36.513
The flip side of that is today.

00:04:36.513 --> 00:04:38.659
Those glaciers, they're melting

00:04:38.659 --> 00:04:40.580
and they're releasing.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:41.770
Whoa

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:43.478
It is the nature of a glacier to melt.

00:04:43.478 --> 00:04:44.453
Glaciers are always melting,

00:04:44.453 --> 00:04:46.975
they've always melted throughout history,

00:04:46.975 --> 00:04:49.480
but what is so different
today is the rate of melt.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:51.104
Never in human history have we ever seen

00:04:51.104 --> 00:04:54.771
glaciers as quick as
they are melting today.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.975
And that's gonna make the sea levels rise.

00:04:59.975 --> 00:05:03.022
So, how do you get
connected to these glaciers?

00:05:03.022 --> 00:05:05.191
If you are living, say, in New Jersey,

00:05:05.191 --> 00:05:07.411
or you're living in Florida,
or Washington state,

00:05:07.411 --> 00:05:09.211
there's going to be more precipitation,

00:05:09.211 --> 00:05:11.808
which means in some places, more snow.

00:05:11.808 --> 00:05:14.001
Or in other places, more rain,

00:05:14.001 --> 00:05:17.326
which means more volume in our rivers

00:05:17.326 --> 00:05:19.826
and our lakes and our marshes.

00:05:22.245 --> 00:05:23.997
The more I started to study glaciers,

00:05:23.997 --> 00:05:25.497
the more people would ask me questions

00:05:25.497 --> 00:05:28.331
that placed that glacier
in a context of people.

00:05:28.331 --> 00:05:32.545
I started to have to have
conversations about fish.

00:05:32.545 --> 00:05:35.405
I started to have to have
conversations about forests.

00:05:35.405 --> 00:05:36.523
And sheep.

00:05:36.523 --> 00:05:41.006
I had to have conversations
about rivers shifting.

00:05:41.006 --> 00:05:43.607
I can look at how people understand

00:05:43.607 --> 00:05:46.154
what is happening here with the ice today

00:05:46.154 --> 00:05:47.652
and I'm able then to understand

00:05:47.652 --> 00:05:50.906
how do people respond to change.

00:05:50.906 --> 00:05:54.055
So many of the young people
that I work with, they are yes,

00:05:54.055 --> 00:05:55.224
climate change is happening,

00:05:55.224 --> 00:05:56.955
yes, the glaciers are melting,

00:05:56.955 --> 00:06:00.800
and yes, individually have the power

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.362
to affect a positive change out of that.

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:04.454
It excites me.

00:06:04.454 --> 00:06:05.527
I will totally be honest with you.

00:06:05.527 --> 00:06:06.793
It really, really does excite me.

00:06:06.793 --> 00:06:09.613
Climate change is really, really hard.

00:06:09.613 --> 00:06:12.126
So I work with young people
and it re-energizes me

00:06:12.126 --> 00:06:15.264
and it makes me excited
and I can stomp back

00:06:15.264 --> 00:06:17.597
into the field re-energized.

00:06:19.546 --> 00:06:22.963
I love, I love being out on the glaciers.

00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:26.687
I really do think that if we lose

00:06:26.687 --> 00:06:30.270
our ice, we lose
something of our humanity.

00:06:31.207 --> 00:06:33.103
We're gonna lose a part of who we are

00:06:33.103 --> 00:06:36.837
if we take away those places where we say,

00:06:36.837 --> 00:06:41.837
wow, where we say, whoa,
or more importantly,

00:06:41.863 --> 00:06:43.427
where we don't say anything at all

00:06:43.427 --> 00:06:45.586
because we're just so blown away.

00:06:45.586 --> 00:06:48.503
(slow piano music)

